Two concerns before merging:

**1. The new HTTP redirect block uses `https://$host$request_uri` — direct IP address access (`http://50.28.86.131/...`) will now redirect to `https://50.28.86.131/...` instead of `https://rustchain.org/...`, causing a TLS certificate error**

The old config had an explicit rule:
```nginx
if ($host = "50.28.86.131") {
    return 301 https://rustchain.org$request_uri;
}
```

This redirected direct IP access to the canonical domain. The new block:
```nginx
server_name rustchain.org www.rustchain.org;
return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
```

Since `server_name` does not include the IP, HTTP requests to `50.28.86.131` on port 80 will now fall through to nginx's default server (likely returning a 404 or the default welcome page) rather than being redirected. If you want to preserve the IP-to-domain redirect, either add the IP to `server_name` with a separate redirect to the canonical hostname, or add a default server block:
```nginx
server {
    listen 80 default_server;
    server_name _;
    return 301 https://rustchain.org$request_uri;
}
```

**2. The cipher list omits ChaCha20-Poly1305 variants, which are preferred for performance on devices without AES hardware acceleration**

The hardened cipher list is:
```
ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384
```

Adding ChaCha20 variants is recommended by Mozilla's modern TLS config and improves performance for mobile clients and low-power hardware:
```
ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-ECDSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384:ECDHE-ECDSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305:ECDHE-RSA-CHACHA20-POLY1305
```

This is particularly relevant for a DePIN network where participants may be running mining nodes on vintage/low-power hardware.

Two concerns before merging:

**1. The semantic route blocks (`/fossil`, `/relic`, `/relics`, `/beacon`, `/anchor`, `/agent`, etc.) have no upstream timeout settings — they can hold nginx worker connections indefinitely**

The new `/api/v1/` and `/api/v2/` blocks correctly include:
```nginx
proxy_connect_timeout 60s;
proxy_send_timeout 60s;
proxy_read_timeout 60s;
```

But every other new location block — `/fossil`, `/relic`, `/relics`, `/beacon`, `/anchor`, `/agent`, and all the others added below those — omits these timeout directives entirely. Without them, nginx falls back to its compiled default (`proxy_read_timeout 60s` in stock builds, but this depends on the server's `nginx.conf` global `proxy_read_timeout` setting). If there is no global `proxy_read_timeout` directive, a hung backend on a `/fossil` request will hold a worker connection until the OS TCP timeout fires (potentially minutes). Please add consistent timeout settings to all new location blocks, or add them once in the `server` context so all locations inherit them.

**2. The new `/healthz`, `/info`, and `/status` endpoints are treated as application routes (full proxy pass with headers) but lack `access_log off`, unlike the existing `/health` endpoint**

The original `/health` block has:
```nginx
location /health {
    proxy_pass http://rustchain_backend/health;
    access_log off;
}
```

The three new health-adjacent endpoints (`/healthz`, `/info`, `/status`) omit `access_log off`. Health and readiness probes are typically called at high frequency by load balancers and orchestrators (Kubernetes, Nomad, AWS ALB, etc.), which will generate substantial log volume. Please add `access_log off;` to these three blocks to match the pattern established by `/health`.

Two concerns before merging:

**1. `.toFixed(6)` always emits trailing zeros — comparison table cells will show `1.500000 RTC` instead of `1.50 RTC` for normal-sized rewards**

`.toFixed(N)` pads to exactly N decimal places regardless of the value. The comparison table column is width-constrained; 6-decimal strings will overflow or truncate on typical screen widths where 2-decimal strings displayed fine. If the goal is to avoid `0.00 RTC` for micro-rewards (very small `baseReward` values), a conditional formatter avoids the overflow without sacrificing precision where it matters:
```javascript
function fmtReward(rtc) {
    if (rtc >= 0.01) return rtc.toFixed(2) + ' RTC';
    return rtc.toPrecision(2) + ' RTC';
}
```

**2. This PR is not coordinated with the open PR #7764, which changes the same file (`mining-simulator.html`) — merging both risks a conflict or inconsistent precision across the same page**

PR #7764 (`[codex] Show VM mining rewards with six decimals`) also changes `.toFixed(2)` → `.toFixed(6)` in `mining-simulator.html` for the hardware tier cards, while this PR changes the comparison table in the same file. If these touch adjacent or overlapping sections, the second merge will produce a conflict.

More importantly, PR #7764 already has a CHANGES_REQUESTED review flagging the trailing-zeros problem and requesting a conditional formatter. If #7764 is updated before this PR merges, the inconsistency between the main card display and the comparison table display will remain until this PR is also updated. The two PRs should either be consolidated into one or explicitly coordinated so both use the same precision logic.

One regression in `formatNumber` before merging:

**The change removes the `M` (millions) display tier, leaving values between 1 million and 999 million formatted as raw localized thousands**

The old implementation had three tiers:
```javascript
if (num >= 1_000_000)   return (num / 1_000_000).toFixed(2) + 'M';
else if (num >= 1_000)  return num.toLocaleString('en-US');
else                    return num.toString();
```

The new implementation has:
```javascript
if (num >= 1_000_000_000) return (num / 1_000_000_000).toFixed(2) + 'B';
else if (num >= 1_000)    return num.toLocaleString('en-US');
else                      return num.toString();
```

Values in the range 1 000 000 – 999 999 999 now fall through to `toLocaleString`, producing strings like `"1,234,567"` or `"123,456,789"` instead of `"1.23M"` or `"123.46M"`. These long strings will overflow compact display containers in the wallet tracker (chart labels, stat cards, table cells) that were sized for the short `"1.23M"` format.

If RTC supply or wallet balance figures are expected to reach the billions range, the fix should preserve the `M` tier and add a `B` tier above it:
```javascript
if (num >= 1_000_000_000)      return (num / 1_000_000_000).toFixed(2) + 'B';
else if (num >= 1_000_000)     return (num / 1_000_000).toFixed(2) + 'M';
else if (num >= 1_000)         return num.toLocaleString('en-US');
else                            return num.toString();
```

If values above 1B are not expected and this change was specifically to handle a case where numbers were incorrectly showing as `"X.XXM"` when they should have been showing as plain thousands, please clarify in the PR description — the current diff gives no context for why the M tier was removed rather than supplemented.

Two concerns before merging:

**1. `calculateDefaultAverageMultiplier()` is called twice on every render cycle but always returns the same value — it should be a module-level constant**

The function reads `DEFAULT_NETWORK`, which is a static constant that never changes at runtime. Calling `Array.reduce` on it twice per page interaction (once at line ~582 for the preset-miner estimate and once at line ~631 for the network-size table) is redundant work.

Replace with a single computation at module scope:
```javascript
const DEFAULT_AVG_MULTIPLIER = (() => {
    const totalWeight = DEFAULT_NETWORK.reduce(
        (sum, item) => sum + item.multiplier * item.count, 0);
    const totalMiners = DEFAULT_NETWORK.reduce(
        (sum, item) => sum + item.count, 0);
    return totalMiners > 0 ? totalWeight / totalMiners : 1.0;
})();
```
This also makes the value visible to anyone reading the file without needing to trace through a function call.

**2. The `totalWeight` variable name inside `calculateDefaultAverageMultiplier()` shadows the `totalWeight` used immediately after the call — different concepts, same name**

Inside the function, `totalWeight` means:
> the sum of (multiplier × count) across all hardware tiers in DEFAULT_NETWORK

Immediately after the call, at the call site, `totalWeight` means:
> the estimated total network hashrate weight: `presetMiners × avgMultiplier + userMultiplier`

These are semantically different quantities with the same identifier. A maintainer reading the two blocks in sequence could easily confuse them, especially since the outer `totalWeight` formula references `avgMultiplier` (the return value of the function) but has the same name as the inner intermediate variable.

Please rename the internal variable — e.g. `weightedMultiplierSum` inside the function — to make the distinction clear:
```javascript
const weightedMultiplierSum = DEFAULT_NETWORK.reduce(
    (sum, item) => sum + item.multiplier * item.count, 0);
const totalMiners = DEFAULT_NETWORK.reduce(
    (sum, item) => sum + item.count, 0);
return totalMiners > 0 ? weightedMultiplierSum / totalMiners : 1.0;
```

Addressed the blocking numeric-field review in commits `bde16bb7` and `4c77585b`:

- normalize `beat_count`, `score` / `maxScore`, `videos`, `totalViews`, and `antiquity_multiplier` with a finite-number helper before interpolation;
- clamp the score-bar value and percentage to a valid 0-100 range;
- extend the XSS regression to reject the remaining raw numeric-looking interpolations and verify the clamp contract.

The PR remains mergeable. Thanks for identifying the remaining aggregation-layer boundary.

Follow-up hardening pushed after reviewing the query plan more critically:

- replaced OFFSET pagination with deterministic keyset pagination over `(value_nrtc, box_id)`;
- added a matching partial composite index for unspent address reads;
- fetches only `limit + 1` rows to derive `has_more`, avoiding full count/materialization on the boxes endpoint;
- added regression coverage for equal-value cursor ties and invalid cursor pairs.

This closes the remaining large-offset scan and sort edge in the initial patch. The PR remains limited to three files and is mergeable.

